"Given the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 5x + 9 = 0\\), we know that the roots \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) satisfy the following relationships:\n\n1. The sum of the roots \\(a + b = 5\\).\n2. The product of the roots \\(ab = 9\\).\n\nWe need to find the value of \\((a - 1)(b - 1)\\).\n\nFirst, let's expand \\((a - 1)(b - 1)\\):\n\n\\[\n(a - 1)(b - 1) = ab - a - b + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the known values of \\(a + b\\) and \\(ab\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\nab - 5 + 1\n\\]\n\nSince \\(ab = 9\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n9 - 5 + 1 = 9 - 5 + 1 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\((a - 1)(b - 1)\\) is \\boxed{5}.).\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(5\\). I hope it is correct."
"To solve the inequality \\(-4 < x^4 + 4x^2 < 21\\), we start by rewriting the inequality in a more convenient form. Notice that \\(x^4 + 4x^2\\) can be factored as \\((x^2 + 2)^2\\). Therefore, the inequality becomes:\n\n\\[\n1 < (x^2 + 2)^2 < 25\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1} < x^2 + 2 < \\sqrt{25}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n1 < x^2 + 2 < 5\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 2 from all parts of the inequality, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n-1 < x^2 < 3\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n-\\sqrt{3} < x < \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the inequality in interval notation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(- \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})}\n\\]"
