"To find the least positive integer in the arithmetic sequence \\(1000, 987, 974, 961, \\ldots\\), we first need to determine the common difference \\(d\\) of the sequence.\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) can be found by subtracting the first term from the second term:\n\\[\nd = 987 - 1000 = -13\n\\]\n\nThe sequence is decreasing by 13 each time. To find the least positive integer in the sequence, we need to find the first term that becomes positive.\n\nStarting from the first term \\(1000\\), we subtract 13 repeatedly:\n\\[\n1000 - 13 = 987\n\\]\n\\[\n987 - 13 = 974\n\\]\n\\[\n974 - 13 = 961\n\\]\n\\[\n961 - 13 = 948\n\\]\n\nThe first term in the sequence that is positive is \\(948\\).\n\nThus, the least positive integer in the sequence is \\(\\boxed{948}\\)."
