"For the quadratic equation \\(9x^2 + nx + 36 = 0\\) to have exactly one solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our equation, \\(a = 9\\), \\(b = n\\), and \\(c = 36\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = n^2 - 4 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 36 = n^2 - 1296\n\\]\n\nFor the equation to have exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\n\\[\nn^2 - 1296 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\nn^2 = 1296\n\\]\n\nTaking the positive square root of both sides gives:\n\n\\[\nn = \\sqrt{1296}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nn = 36\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{36}\\)."
"To simplify the expression \\((5-3i)(-4+3i)\\), we use the distributive property (also known as the FOIL method for binomials):\n\n\\[\n(5-3i)(-4+3i) = 5(-4) + 5(3i) - 3i(-4) + 3i(3i)\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\n= -20 + 15i + 12i + 9i^2\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(i^2 = -1\\), so:\n\n\\[\n= -20 + 15i + 12i + 9(-1)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n= -20 + 15i + 12i - 9\n\\]\n\nCombine the real parts and the imaginary parts:\n\n\\[\n= (-20 - 9) + (15i + 12i)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= -29 + 27i\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{-29 + 27i}\\)."
