"Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nWe are given two equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 25 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 9 \\)\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously. First, we add the two equations to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 25 + 9\n\\]\n\\[\n2x = 34\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 17\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 17 \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( y \\). Let's use the first equation:\n\\[\n17 + y = 25\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 25 - 17\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 8\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( x = 17 \\) and \\( y = 8 \\), we can find their product:\n\\[\nx \\cdot y = 17 \\cdot 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\cdot y = 136\n\\]\nTherefore, the product of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{136}\\)."
