"To find the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{1 - \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{x}}} \\), we need to ensure that the expressions inside all square roots are non-negative.\n\n1. The innermost square root is \\( \\sqrt{x} \\). For this to be defined, \\( x \\geq 0 \\).\n\n2. The next square root is \\( \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{x}} \\). For this to be defined, \\( 2 - \\sqrt{x} \\geq 0 \\). Solving this inequality, we get \\( \\sqrt{x} \\leq 2 \\), which implies \\( x \\leq 4 \\).\n\n3. The outermost square root is \\( \\sqrt{1 - \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{x}}} \\). For this to be defined, \\( 1 - \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{x}} \\geq 0 \\). This inequality is always true given that \\( x \\leq 4 \\).\n\nCombining these conditions, we have \\( 0 \\leq x \\leq 4 \\).\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{[0, 4]}\\)."
