"Let's denote the number of quarters in the third pile as \\( x \\).\n\n1. The second pile has \\( x + 4 \\) quarters.\n2. The first pile has \\( (x + 4) - 5 = x - 1 \\) quarters.\n3. The fourth pile has \\( 3(x + 4) = 3x + 12 \\) quarters.\n\nThe total number of quarters is the sum of the quarters in all four piles:\n\\[\n(x - 1) + (x + 4) + x + (3x + 12) = 27\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[\n6x + 15 = 27\n\\]\n\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[\n6x = 12\n\\]\n\nDivide by 6:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, the number of quarters in the fourth pile is:\n\\[\n3x + 12 = 3(2) + 12 = 6 + 12 = 18\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the fourth pile has \\(\\boxed{18}\\) quarters."
