"To find \\( f(\\pi) \\), we need to determine which piece of the piecewise function applies to \\( x = \\pi \\).\n\nThe function \\( f(x) \\) is defined as follows:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\begin{cases}\n-x^2 - 1 & \\text{if } x < 0, \\\\\n2 & \\text{if } 0 \\le x < 4, \\\\\n\\sqrt{x} & \\text{if } x \\ge 4.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\pi \\) is greater than 4, we use the third piece of the function, which is \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x} \\).\n\nNow, we substitute \\( x = \\pi \\) into this piece of the function:\n\\[\nf(\\pi) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(\\pi) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\)."
"To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt[3]{x\\sqrt{x}}=7\\), we start by simplifying the left-hand side. Recall that \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) can be written as \\(x^{1/2}\\). Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x \\cdot x^{1/2}} = 7\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the cube root:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x^{3/2}} = 7\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt[3]{x^{3/2}} = x^{1/2}\\), the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx^{1/2} = 7\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we need to eliminate the exponent. We do this by raising both sides of the equation to the power of 2:\n\n\\[\n(x^{1/2})^2 = 7^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = 49\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\nx = 49\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{49}\\)."
"To find the distance from the point \\((x, y)\\) to the origin, we need to find \\(\\sqrt {x^2 + y^2}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x + y = 13 \\]\n\\[ xy = 24 \\]\n\nWe can use the identity:\n\\[ (x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy \\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\\[ 13^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2 \\cdot 24 \\]\n\\[ 169 = x^2 + y^2 + 48 \\]\n\nSolve for \\(x^2 + y^2\\):\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 169 - 48 \\]\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 121 \\]\n\nTherefore, the distance from the point \\((x, y)\\) to the origin is:\n\\[ \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = \\sqrt{121} = 11 \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{11}\\)."
"To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\dots\\), we can use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence. The formula is:\n\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nIn this sequence, the first term \\(a\\) is 1, and the common difference \\(d\\) is 2. We need to find the 2003rd term, so \\(n = 2003\\).\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + (2003-1) \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 2004 \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 4008 \\]\n\nTherefore, the 2003rd term is \\boxed{4009}."
