"To solve the inequality \\((x-5)(x+5)<0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the product of the two factors is negative. This occurs when one factor is positive and the other is negative.\n\nLet's analyze the factors \\((x-5)\\) and \\((x+5)\\):\n\n1. \\((x-5)\\) is positive when \\(x > 5\\) and negative when \\(x < 5\\).\n2. \\((x+5)\\) is positive when \\(x < -5\\) and negative when \\(x > -5\\).\n\nThe product \\((x-5)(x+5)\\) is negative when one factor is positive and the other is negative. This happens in the intervals where \\((x-5)\\) is positive and \\((x+5)\\) is negative, or vice versa.\n\nThus, we are interested in the interval \\(-5 < x < 5\\).\n\nThe smallest integer in this interval is \\(-6\\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest integer that satisfies the inequality \\((x-5)(x+5)<0\\) is \\(\\boxed{-6}\\)."
