"To determine how many squares can be formed with 4 of the 200 points as vertices, we need to consider the number of ways to choose 4 points out of 200. This is a combination problem, where the order in which we choose the points does not matter.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 4 points out of 200 is given by the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{200}{4} = \\frac{200!}{4! \\cdot 196!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{200}{4} = \\frac{200 \\times 199 \\times 198 \\times 197}{4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{200 \\times 199 \\times 198 \\times 197}{24} = 382,350\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of squares that can be formed with 4 of the 200 points as vertices is $\\boxed{382350}$."
"To determine the number of ways to select 5 students from a group of 6 students, we use the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of students, and \\( k \\) is the number of students to be selected.\n\nHere, \\( n = 6 \\) and \\( k = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{6!}{5!(6-5)!} = \\frac{6!}{5! \\cdot 1!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 1! = 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{6!}{5!}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\( 6! = 720 \\) and \\( 5! = 120 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{5} = \\frac{720}{120} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to select 5 students out of a group of 6 students is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
