"To determine the number of ways to fill the committee, we need to select one $7^{\\text{th}}$-grader and three $8^{\\text{th}}$-graders from the available candidates.\n\nFirst, we choose one $7^{\\text{th}}$-grader from the four available $7^{\\text{th}}$-graders. There are 4 choices for this selection.\n\nNext, we choose three $8^{\\text{th}}$-graders from the six available $8^{\\text{th}}$-graders. The number of ways to choose 3 out of 6 is given by the combination formula $\\binom{6}{3} =\n\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 20$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to fill the committee is the product of the choices for selecting the $7^{\\text{th}}$-grader and the $8^{\\text{th}}$-grader: $4 \\times 20 = \\boxed{80}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $80$. I hope it is correct."
"The expected value \\( E \\) of a random variable is calculated by summing the products of each outcome and its probability. Here, the outcomes are heads, tails, and edge, with their respective probabilities and winnings.\n\nThe expected value \\( E \\) is given by:\n\\[\nE = \\left( \\frac{3}{5} \\times 4 \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{5} \\times (-1) \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{5} \\times (-10) \\right)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{5} \\times 4 = \\frac{12}{5} = 2.4\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} \\times (-1) = -\\frac{1}{5} = -0.2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} \\times (-10) = -2\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together:\n\\[\nE = 2.4 + (-0.2) - 2 = 0.2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the expected winnings from one flip is \\(\\boxed{0.20}\\)."
"To determine the probability that the product of the numbers on the five visible faces of a fair six-sided die is divisible by 6, we need to consider the conditions under which the product is divisible by 6. A product is divisible by 6 if and only if it includes at least one factor of 2 and one factor of 3.\n\nThe numbers on a six-sided die are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. Among these, the numbers 2 and 4 are divisible by 2, and the numbers 3 and 6 are divisible by 3. Therefore, the product of the numbers on the five visible faces will be divisible by 6 if at least one of the visible faces is a 2 or a 4, and at least one of the visible faces is a 3 or a 6.\n\nSince the die is fair, each face has an equal probability of being visible. The probability that any given visible face is a 2 or a 4 is $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nThus, the probability that the product of the numbers on the five visible faces is divisible by 6 is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}$."
