"To determine the number of possible quintets consisting of three boys and two girls, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 8 and 2 girls out of 6.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 8:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{3} = \\frac{8!}{3!(8-3)!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 56\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 6:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of possible quintets, we multiply the number of ways to choose the boys by the number of ways to choose the girls:\n\\[\n56 \\times 15 = 840\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of possible quintets that can be formed is \\(\\boxed{840}\\)."
