"To find the area of the circle circumscribed about an equilateral triangle, we first need to determine the radius of the circumscribed circle. The formula for the radius \\( R \\) of the circumscribed circle of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( a \\) is:\n\n\\[ R = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}} \\]\n\nGiven that the side length \\( a \\) of the equilateral triangle is 6 units, we can substitute this value into the formula:\n\n\\[ R = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}} = 2\\sqrt{3} \\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the area \\( A \\) of a circle, which is:\n\n\\[ A = \\pi R^2 \\]\n\nSubstituting the radius \\( R = 2\\sqrt{3} \\) into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ A = \\pi (2\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\pi \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = 12\\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{12\\pi} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 12\\pi \\). I hope it is correct."
