"To compute \\(\\tan 420^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that the tangent function has a period of \\(180^\\circ\\). This means that \\(\\tan(\\theta + 180^\\circ) = \\tan(\\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(420^\\circ\\) modulo \\(180^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n420^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 240^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(\\tan 420^\\circ = \\tan 240^\\circ\\).\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\tan 240^\\circ\\). The angle \\(240^\\circ\\) is in the third quadrant, where the tangent function is negative. The reference angle for \\(240^\\circ\\) is \\(240^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 60^\\circ\\), and \\(\\tan 60^\\circ = 1\\).\n\nTherefore, \n\n\\[\n\\sin 240^\\circ = -\\tan 60^\\circ = -1\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\)."
"To compute \\(\\cos(-150^\\circ)\\), we can use the properties of the cosine function and the unit circle.\n\nFirst, note that \\(-150^\\circ\\) is in the fourth quadrant, where the cosine function is positive. The reference angle for \\(-150^\\circ\\) is the positive angle it makes with the positive x-axis. To find this reference angle, we add \\(180^\\circ\\) to \\(-150^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n-150^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSo, the reference angle is \\(30^\\circ\\). The cosine of \\(30^\\circ\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSince \\(-150^\\circ\\) is in the fourth quadrant, the cosine of \\(-150^\\circ\\) will be the same as the cosine of its reference angle \\(30^\\circ\\).:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(-150^\\circ) = \\cos(30^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)."
"To find the mean of the measures of the three exterior angles of a triangle, we first need to determine the measures of the exterior angles. The sum of the interior angles in a triangle is 180 degrees. Given two interior angles of 63 degrees and 78 degrees, we can find the third interior angle:\n\n\\[\n180 - 63 - 78 = 39 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\nThe exterior angles of a triangle are supplementary to the interior angles. Therefore, the exterior angles are:\n\n\\[\n180 - 63 = 117 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\\[\n180 - 78 = 92 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\\[\n180 - 39 = 141 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\nThe mean of these exterior angles is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{117 + 92 + 141}{3} = \\frac{350}{3} = 110 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( \\boxed{110} \\) degrees."
"To compute \\(\\sin 135^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that \\(135^\\circ\\) is in the second quadrant, where the sine function is positive. The reference angle for \\(135^\\circ\\) is the angle between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(90^\\circ\\), which is \\(180^\\circ - 135^\\circ = 45^\\circ\\).\n\nThe sine of the reference angle \\(45^\\circ\\) is \\(\\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nSince \\(135^\\circ\\) is in the second quadrant, the sine of \\(135^\\circ\\) is positive and equal to the sine of the reference angle.\n\nTherefore, \\(\\sin 135^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). I hope it is correct."
