"Let the dimensions of the rectangular box be \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). The volume of the box is given by \\(abc = 216\\). The surface area of the box is given by \\(2(ab + bc + ca)\\).\n\nTo minimize the surface area, we need to minimize \\(ab + bc + ca\\). By the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\\[\nab + bc + ca \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{abc}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(abc = 216\\), we get:\n\\[\nabc ca \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{216} = 3 \\cdot 6 = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(ab + bc + ca\\) is 18. which occurs when \\(a = b = c = 6\\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the box is:\n\\[\n2(ab + bc + ca) = 2 \\cdot 18 = 36\n\\]\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{36}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $36$. I hope it is correct."
