"Let $A = 3k$ and $B = 7k$, where $k$ is a positive integer. The least common multiple of $A$ and $B$ is given by the formula:\n\n$$\\text{LCM}(A, B) = \\frac{A \\cdot B}{\\text{GCD}(A, B)}.$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have:\n\n$$\\1575}(3k)(7k)}{\\text{GCD}(3k, 7k)}.$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$\\1575 = \\frac{21k^2}{\\text{GCD}(3k, 7k)}.$$\n\nSince $k$ is a positive integer, we can divide both sides by $k^2$:\n\n$$\\1575 = \\frac{21}{\\text{GCD}(3k, 7k)}.$$\n\nSolving for the GCD:\n\n$$\\text{GCD}(3k, 7k) = \\frac{21}{1575} = 1.$$\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of $A$ and $B$ is $\\boxed{1}$."
