"To find the value of \\( x \\) given the equations \\( 2x + 3y = 4 \\) and \\( y = 34 \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute the value of \\( y \\) into the first equation.\n2. Solve for \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\( y = 34 \\) into the equation \\( 2x + 3y = 4 \\):\n\n\\[ 2x + 3(34) = 4 \\]\n\nNext, perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[ 2x + 102 = 4 \\]\n\nNow, isolate the term with \\( x \\) by subtracting 102 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ 2x = 4 - 102 \\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ 2x = -98 \\]\n\nFinally, solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides by 2:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-98}{2} \\]\n\n\\[ x = -49 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{-49}\\)."
"To determine the number of ways to select 2 balls from a bin of 20 balls where the order does not matter, we use the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( n = 20 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20!}{2!(20-2)!} = \\frac{20!}{2! \\cdot 18!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\( 18! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20 \\times 19 \\times 18!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 18!} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{380}{2} = 190\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to select 2 balls is: \\\n\\boxed{190}"
