"We know that $\\tan 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ and $\\tan 40^\\circ = 1$. Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 40^\\circ + \\sqrt{3} \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 1.\\]\n\nSimplifying each term, we get:\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + 1 + 1.\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + 1 + 1 = 1 + 1 = \\boxed{2}.\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $2$. I hope it is correct."
